{"records":[{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"42sxkx9sdb","title":"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets. Pal et al","description":"Supplementary data for the paper: \"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets\". \nTable S1: Enriched Reactome pathways for DE gene sets (FDR<0.1). (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 2).\nTable S2: List of synthetic lethal pairs in each of the transcriptomic dataset used in this study and the list of unified 454 SL/SDL partner genes. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1).\nTable S3: List of 140 candidate targets and three subsets comprising known drug targets, hub SL/SDL partner genes when each one of them are paired up with multiple DE genes and genes with enriched Reactome pathways.  Pathway enrichment analysis (FDR <0.1) for SL/SDL partner genes, DE genes and combination pathway enrichment analysis for DE gene-SL/SDL partner gene pairs for 140 candidates. List of 135 candidates overlapping with genome-wide siRNA screen. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1 and 2).\nTable S4: Targeted siRNA screen results for selected 26 targets and annotation of 26 candidate targets with reference to SARS-CoV-2 or any other virus in the literature. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 3).\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-19T18:44:35.774Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/42sxkx9sdb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/42sxkx9sdb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lipika","last_name":"R. Pal","orcid":"0000-0002-3390-110X"},{"first_name":"Kuoyuan","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nishanth Ulhas","last_name":"Nair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Martin-Sancho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanju","last_name":"Sinha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Pu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Riva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fiorella","last_name":"Schischlik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joo Sang","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sumit K.","last_name":"Chanda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eytan","last_name":"Ruppin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptome","Drug","Antiviral Therapy","CRISPR/Cas9","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4224kkbrry","title":"Analysis scripts for the paper: The Skin-Brain Axis: Shared Genetic Architecture Between Immune-Mediated Skin Diseases and Emotional/Behavioral Disorders","description":"STAR★METHODS\n\tGWAS Data Sources\n\tGlobal and Local Genetic Correlation Analysis \n\tMulti-trait Association Analysis\n\tFunctional Annotation\n\tGene Mapping Analysis\n\tGenome-Wide Gene-based and Gene-set Analyses\n\tTranscriptome-Wide Association Analysis \n\tLinkage Disequilibrium Score Regression Applied to Specifically Expressed Genes (LDSC-SEG)\n\tTissue and Cell Type Specificity Analysis\n\tMendelian Randomization to Identify Potential Causal Plasma Protein Targets\n\tSoftware\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-30T15:12:57.987Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4224kkbrry","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4224kkbrry"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yunxuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Code Reuse"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d9ffb7kkzt","title":"Transcriptomic Dataset for Network based identification of key Master Regulators for Immunologic Constant of Rejection","description":"The goal of this study was to identify molecular alterations governing mechanisms for the immune exclusion phenotype. We developed a network-based approach to identify key transcription factors (TF) associated with poor immunologic anti-tumor activity. Based on the Immunologic Constant of Rejection (ICR) signature, tumors may be classified as immune active (ICR High) or immune silent (ICR Low). \nWe used The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) RNA-seq data of 12 specific cancer types (2,307 samples, 3,674 TFs, and 23,216 target genes) to build gene regulatory networks, determine each TF’s regulon,  followed by determination of activity matrix of TFs for all tumor samples, and finally run a fast gene-set enrichment analysis to identify the most important TFs, named Master Regulators (MR), that are unique to ICR Low and ICR High tumors respectively. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-01T09:38:28.801Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d9ffb7kkzt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d9ffb7kkzt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Raghvendra","last_name":"Mall","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptomics","Expression Array"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hnh3hzvb7p","title":"early duodenal cancer","description":"Figures and Tables for the manuscript, 'Proteome- and genome- wide profiling of early duodenal cancer'.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-26T14:45:47.672Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hnh3hzvb7p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hnh3hzvb7p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lingling","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Early Cancer Detection","Proteogenomics","Duodenal Ulcer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfn4ryk7fn","title":"Mass Spectrometry data related to \"miRNA data related to \"Organ-specific isolation of hepatocyte extracellular vesicles from human plasma enables tissue-resolved proteomic and miRNA profiling\"","description":"Mass Spectrometry metadata and intensity data related to \"miRNA data related to \"Organ-specific isolation of hepatocyte extracellular vesicles from human plasma enables tissue-resolved proteomic and miRNA profiling\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-05T21:18:05.629Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfn4ryk7fn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfn4ryk7fn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ricardo","last_name":"Figueiras","orcid":"0009-0000-8203-8229"},{"first_name":"Susana","last_name":"Vagueiro","orcid":"0000-0002-5960-2597"},{"first_name":"Raissa","last_name":"Kay","orcid":"0000-0002-6842-8316"},{"first_name":"Elnaz","last_name":"Persia","orcid":"0009-0008-4801-6964"},{"first_name":"Roger","last_name":"de Alwis","orcid":"0000-0002-3520-3575"},{"first_name":"Razan","last_name":"Hijazi","orcid":"0009-0006-7594-6265"},{"first_name":"Raquel","last_name":"Sanches-Kuiper","orcid":"0009-0003-4525-2084"},{"first_name":"Kristine","last_name":"von Bargen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Davidson","orcid":"0000-0002-9152-5907"},{"first_name":"Pierre","last_name":"Arsene","orcid":"0000-0002-8870-1295"},{"first_name":"Tomas","last_name":"Dias","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Extracellular Vesicle"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":126991,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":114,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":126991,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":126398,"restricted":66,"embargoed":527},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2172},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":660},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":446},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":386},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":312},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":253},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":216}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":124470},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1810},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Fl